<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 4.1 Functions of Several Variables.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  ch-14-1.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id here is prefixed
  with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: the surfaces are three-dimensional plots no reader can draw by
  hand in a lecture, so they are reproduced in full, as in the lecture
  notes, and the reader is asked to read properties off them.  The two
  contour maps ARE the answer to their exercise, so they become empty
  grids to draw the level curves on.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-fmv-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Functions of Several Variables</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Evaluate a function <m>z = f(x,y)</m> of two variables, describe its
domain as a region of the plane, and recognize its graph as a surface in
space.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find and sketch the level curves <m>f(x,y) = c</m> of a function of two
variables, and read a contour map.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Explain the relationship between the level curves drawn on the surface
and the contour map drawn in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the level surfaces <m>f(x,y,z) = c</m> of a function of three
variables.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
So far we have studied functions that take one real number as input and
return one real number as output. Many quantities, however, depend on
more than one input: the temperature at a point of a metal plate depends
on two coordinates, and the temperature in a room depends on three. In
this section we study functions of two variables, whose graphs are
surfaces in space, and we develop two ways of visualizing them, namely
level curves and contour maps. At the end we look at functions of three
variables, which we visualize by their level surfaces.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-fsv-functions-two-variables">
<title>Functions of Two Variables</title>

<p>
As an example consider <m>z = f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m>. This function
takes a pair of real numbers <m>x, y \in \R</m> as its input and as an
output gives a real number <m>z \in \R</m>. Evaluate it at the point
<m>P_0(0.5, 0.5)</m>, and describe its domain. (Substituting is one line.
For the domain, ask which pairs <m>(x,y)</m> the formula accepts, and then
which of those keep <m>z</m> from going negative <mdash/> the disk drawn in
<xref ref="fig-skel-fsv-mapping"/> is the second of these.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$f(0.5,0.5) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Domain:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the value of the function at the given point and one for its domain.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-fsv-mapping">
<caption>
The function <m>f</m> takes a point <m>(x,y)</m> of the disk
<m>x^2+y^2 \le 1</m> and returns the single number <m>z = f(x,y)</m>. The
point <m>P_0(0.5,0.5)</m> is sent to <m>f(0.5,0.5) = 0.5</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-fsv-mapping">
<diagram dimensions="(600,270)" margins="12">
<definition>bt(t) = ((1-t)^2*1.05 + 2*t*(1-t)*4.0 + t^2*7.0, (1-t)^2*1.25 + 2*t*(1-t)*3.15 + t^2*0.42)</definition>
<definition>bb(t) = ((1-t)^2*0.28 + 2*t*(1-t)*3.3 + t^2*6.55, (1-t)^2*(-0.62) + 2*t*(1-t)*(-2.05) + t^2*0.05)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-2.6,9.4,3.5)">
<circle at="fm-disk" center="(0,0)" radius="1.9"
        fill="#7F7F7F" fill-opacity="0.28" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<vector at="fm-xaxis" v="(4.15,0)" tail="(-2.1,0)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fm-yaxis" v="(0,5.0)" tail="(0,-2.2)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fm-zaxis" v="(4.0,0)" tail="(5.0,0.25)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="fm-top" function="bt" domain="(0,0.95)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fm-top-arrow" v="(0.300,-0.261)" tail="(6.700,0.681)"
        stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="fm-bot" function="bb" domain="(0,0.95)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fm-bot-arrow" v="(0.324,0.201)" tail="(6.226,-0.151)"
        stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="2"/>
<point at="fm-P0" p="(0.956,0.956)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<point at="fm-P" p="(-0.674,-1.165)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="fm-fP0" p="(7.35,0.25)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<point at="fm-fP" p="(6.55,0.25)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<label at="fm-disk-lab" anchor="(-1.0,2.0)" alignment="n">
<m>x^2+y^2\le 1</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-x" anchor="(2.1,-0.15)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="fm-y" anchor="(0.15,2.8)" alignment="e"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="fm-z" anchor="(9.15,0.25)" alignment="e"><m>z</m></label>
<label at="fm-P0-lab" anchor="(0.883,1.103)" alignment="nw" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>P_0(0.5,0.5)</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-P-lab" anchor="(-0.674,-1.03)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2">
<m>P(x,y)</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-fP0-lab" anchor="(7.45,-0.05)" alignment="se" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>f(0.5,0.5)=0.5</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-fP-lab" anchor="(6.4,0.5)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>z=f(x,y)</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-f1" anchor="(6.85,0.55)" alignment="n" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>f</m>
</label>
<label at="fm-f2" anchor="(6.35,-0.25)" alignment="s" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>f</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="On the left, a shaded disk in the x y plane represents the domain, the set of points with x squared plus y squared at most one. Two points are marked in it: P nought at zero point five, zero point five, and a general point P. On the right is a horizontal z axis carrying the two output values. Dashed grey arrows labelled f run from each input point to its output value.">
  <annotation ref="fm-disk" text="The domain of f: the disk of radius one, consisting of all points with x squared plus y squared at most one."/>
  <annotation ref="fm-P0" text="The sample input point P nought with coordinates zero point five, zero point five."/>
  <annotation ref="fm-fP0" text="Its output, f of zero point five and zero point five, which equals zero point five, marked on the z axis."/>
  <annotation ref="fm-P" text="A general input point P with coordinates x and y."/>
  <annotation ref="fm-fP" text="Its output z equals f of x y on the z axis."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
If we repeat this process for all the points inside the disk, we have a
set of points in 3D of the form <m>P(x, y, f(x,y))</m>, which represent a
surface known as a <term>paraboloid</term>. The surface is the
<term>graph</term> of the function, and it is drawn in
<xref ref="fig-skel-fsv-paraboloid"/>. Notice that the point
<m>P_0(0.5,0.5)</m> of the domain sits in the <m>xy</m>-plane, while the
corresponding point of the graph lies directly above it.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-fsv-paraboloid">
<caption>
The graph of <m>z = f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> over the disk
<m>x^2+y^2 \le 1</m>. The point <m>P_0(0.5,0.5,0)</m> of the domain lies
in the <m>xy</m>-plane and the corresponding point
<m>P(0.5,0.5,0.5)</m> lies on the surface directly above it.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A dome shaped surface, the graph of z equals one minus x squared minus y
squared, sitting above a shaded disk in the x y plane. A dashed vertical
segment joins the point P nought in the disk to the point P on the
surface directly above it.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11cm, height=8.5cm,
  view={-35}{20},
  xmin=-1.35, xmax=1.35, ymin=-1.35, ymax=1.35, zmin=0, zmax=1.15,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-1,0,1}, ytick={-1,0,1}, ztick={0,0.5,1},
  clip=false,
]
% the disk in the xy-plane
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=gray!35, draw=gray!45,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.3,
          domain=0:1, y domain=0:360, samples=12, samples y=37]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{0});
% the paraboloid z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 over the disk
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=gray!55, draw=gray!60,
          fill opacity=0.55, draw opacity=0.4,
          domain=0:1, y domain=0:360, samples=16, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{1-x^2});
% the rim of the disk
\addplot3[very thick, blue!65!black, domain=0:360, samples=90]
  ({cos(x)},{sin(x)},{0});
% P0 in the plane and P on the surface
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7, color=purple!70!black]
  coordinates {(0.5,0.5,0)};
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7, color=purple!70!black]
  coordinates {(0.5,0.5,0.5)};
\draw[gray, dashed, thick] (axis cs:0.5,0.5,0) -- (axis cs:0.5,0.5,0.5);
\node[purple!70!black, below] at (axis cs:0.5,0.52,-0.02) {$P_0$};
\node[purple!70!black, above right] at (axis cs:0.5,0.5,0.5) {$P$};
\node[blue!65!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.16)
  {$x^2+y^2=1$};
\node[gray!30!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.62)
  {$z=1-x^2-y^2$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Read the coordinates of that point of the graph off
<xref ref="fig-skel-fsv-paraboloid"/>, and say in one line how the graph
of a function of two variables differs from the graph of a function of one
variable. (The third coordinate is the number you computed above. For the
comparison, count dimensions: what does the input live in, and what does
the graph live in?)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$P =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {A graph of one variable is a \dots, of two variables a \dots};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the coordinates of the point on the surface and one for the comparison between graphs of one and of two variables.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-fsv-level-curves">
<title>Level Curves and Contour Maps</title>

<p>
Functions of two variables can be visualized by means of
<term>level curves</term>. These are curves that connect points where the
function <m>z = f(x,y)</m> takes on a given value <m>c</m>. Take again
<m>z = f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> and find its level curves. (Set
<m>f(x,y) = c</m> and rearrange until only <m>x^2 + y^2</m> is on the
left; the shape should then be recognizable at a glance. Say what curve
you get and what its radius is, and note for which values of <m>c</m>
there is no curve at all.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2.5) -- (15,2.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {Setting $f(x,y) = c$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {The curve is a \dots\ of radius \dots};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.2) {No curve when:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the equation of the level curve, for the shape and radius, and for the values of c that give no curve.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-fsv-level-curves">
<title>Level Curves of <m>f(x,y) = 1-x^2-y^2</m></title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-fsv-level-curves" number="yes">
1 - x^2 - y^2 = c
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
x^2 + y^2 = 1 - c.
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Fill in the radius for each value of <m>c</m> below, then look at
<xref ref="fig-skel-fsv-level-curves-3d"/>, where these curves are drawn
on the surface itself as intersections with the horizontal planes
<m>z = c</m>. (Notice which way round it goes: larger <m>c</m> gives the
<em>smaller</em> circle. Say why, from the shape of the surface.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.2) {$c=-2:$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (5.35,7.2) {$c=-1:$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (10.35,7.2) {$c=0:$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {$c=\tfrac12:$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (5.35,5.2) {$c=\tfrac34:$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Why larger $c$ gives a smaller circle:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with five slots for the radii at the given values of c and a row for why a larger value of c gives a smaller circle.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-fsv-level-curves-3d">
<caption>
Level curves of <m>f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> drawn on the surface. The
curve at height <m>c</m> is the intersection of the graph with the
horizontal plane <m>z = c</m>; higher values of <m>c</m> give smaller
circles.
</caption>
<image width="76%">
<description>
<p>
A dome shaped surface with several horizontal circles drawn on it at
different heights, each in a different colour. The circles are small near
the top of the dome and grow larger further down.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11.5cm, height=9.5cm,
  view={-35}{18},
  xmin=-1.9, xmax=1.9, ymin=-1.9, ymax=1.9, zmin=-2.4, zmax=1.3,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-1,0,1}, ytick={-1,0,1}, ztick={1,0,-1,-2},
  clip=false,
]
% the surface, drawn down to z = -2.2
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=gray!45, draw=gray!55,
          fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.3,
          domain=0:1.79, y domain=0:360, samples=13, samples y=31,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{1-x^2});
% level curves at c = 3/4, 1/2, 0, -1, -2  (radii sqrt(1-c))
\addplot3[very thick, orange, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.5*cos(x)},{0.5*sin(x)},{0.75});
\addplot3[very thick, purple!70!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.7071*cos(x)},{0.7071*sin(x)},{0.5});
\addplot3[very thick, cyan!60!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({cos(x)},{sin(x)},{0});
\addplot3[very thick, green!50!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({1.4142*cos(x)},{1.4142*sin(x)},{-1});
\addplot3[very thick, black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({1.7321*cos(x)},{1.7321*sin(x)},{-2});
% labels
\node[orange, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.86)
  {$z=3/4$};
\node[purple!70!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.76)
  {$z=1/2$};
\node[cyan!60!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.66)
  {$z=0$};
\node[green!50!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.44)
  {$z=-1$};
\node[black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.22)
  {$z=-2$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Projecting those level curves down into the <m>xy</m>-plane gives the
<term>contour map</term> (or <term>contour plot</term>) of the function.
Draw it on the grid below, labelling each circle with its value of
<m>c</m>. (A contour map is flat but still records the shape of the
surface: after drawing it, say what close-together contours mean and what
far-apart ones mean.)
</p>

<sidebyside widths="46% 46%">
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-2.1,-2.1) grid[step=0.5] (2.1,2.1);
\draw[->, thick] (-2.1,0) -- (2.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2.1) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2} \draw (\x,-0.08) -- (\x,0.08) node[below=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2} \draw (-0.08,\y) -- (0.08,\y) node[left=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid running from negative two to two in both directions, for drawing the contour map of the function.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (7,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (7,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.7) {Contours close together:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,3.2) {Contours far apart:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, for what closely spaced contours indicate and what widely spaced contours indicate.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-fsv-level-curves">
<title>Sketching Level Curves</title>
<statement>
<p>
Sketch some level curves of the function
<m>g(x,y) = x^2 + \dfrac{y^2}{4}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Set <m>g(x,y) = c</m>. For <m>c \gt 0</m> the result is a conic you met
in <xref ref="sec-skel-geom-conics"/>; name it, and give its two semi-axes
in terms of <m>c</m>. Then sketch the curves for several values of
<m>c</m> on the grid, and say which direction they are stretched in and by
how much. What happens when <m>c = 0</m>, and when <m>c \lt 0</m>?)
</p>

<sidebyside widths="52% 40%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (8,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7.5) -- (8,7.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (8,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,10.7) {Setting $g(x,y)=c$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,7.2) {Shape, and its semi-axes:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,3.7) {$c = 0$: \qquad\qquad $c \lt 0$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the equation of the level curve, for its shape and semi-axes, and for the two degenerate cases.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-1.5,-2.4) grid[step=0.25] (1.5,2.4);
\draw[->, thick] (-1.5,0) -- (1.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2.4) -- (0,2.7) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1} \draw (\x,-0.07) -- (\x,0.07) node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2} \draw (-0.07,\y) -- (0.07,\y) node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid taller than it is wide, running from negative one and a half to one and a half horizontally and negative two and a half to two and a half vertically, for sketching the elliptical level curves.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
The surface <m>z = g(x,y)</m> itself is shown in
<xref ref="fig-skel-fsv-elliptic-paraboloid"/>; it is the
<term>elliptic paraboloid</term> of
<xref ref="subsec-skel-elliptical-paraboloid"/>. Use it to check the
curves you drew.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-fsv-elliptic-paraboloid">
<caption>
The elliptic paraboloid <m>z = g(x,y) = x^2 + \frac{y^2}{4}</m>. Slicing
it with a horizontal plane <m>z = c</m> produces the ellipses you drew
above.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A bowl shaped surface opening upwards, wider in the y direction than in
the x direction, with several horizontal elliptical rings drawn on it at
different heights.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11cm, height=8.5cm,
  view={-40}{18},
  xmin=-1.25, xmax=1.25, ymin=-2.5, ymax=2.5, zmin=0, zmax=1.2,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-1,0,1}, ytick={-2,0,2}, ztick={0,0.5,1},
  clip=false,
]
% surface z = x^2 + y^2/4, drawn in "elliptic polar" coordinates so the
% domain is exactly the region where z <= 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=green!45!black, draw=green!35!black,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=0:1, y domain=0:360, samples=13, samples y=31,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({x*cos(y)},{2*x*sin(y)},{x^2});
% a few level ellipses on the surface
\addplot3[very thick, green!25!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.4472*cos(x)},{0.8944*sin(x)},{0.2});
\addplot3[very thick, green!45!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.6325*cos(x)},{1.2649*sin(x)},{0.4});
\addplot3[very thick, green!60!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({0.7746*cos(x)},{1.5492*sin(x)},{0.6});
\addplot3[very thick, yellow!70!black, domain=0:360, samples=48]
  ({cos(x)},{2*sin(x)},{1});
\node[green!35!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.02,0.72)
  {$z=x^2+y^2/4$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-fsv-level-surfaces">
<title>More Than Two Variables: Level Surfaces</title>

<p>
It is not possible to draw the graph of a function of more than two
variables. The graph of a function <m>f(x,y,z)</m> would consist of the
set of points <m>(x, y, z, f(x,y,z))</m> in four-dimensional space
<m>\R^4</m>. However, it is possible to draw the
<term>level surfaces</term> of a function of three variables
<m>f(x,y,z)</m>. These are the surfaces with equation
<md xml:id="eq-skel-fsv-level-surface" number="yes">
f(x,y,z) = c.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Identify the level surfaces of <m>f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2</m>, then
check your answer against <xref ref="fig-skel-fsv-level-spheres"/>. (Write
out <xref ref="eq-skel-fsv-level-surface"/> for this <m>f</m> and compare
it with <xref ref="eq-skel-3d-sphere"/>. Give the radius in terms of
<m>c</m>, and say what happens for <m>c \lt 0</m> and for <m>c = 0</m>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {The level surfaces are \dots\ of radius \dots};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$c = 0$: \qquad\qquad $c \lt 0$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the family of level surfaces and their radius and one for the two degenerate cases.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-fsv-level-spheres">
<caption>
The level surfaces of <m>f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2</m> are spheres. Shown
here are <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 1</m>, <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 4</m>, and
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 9</m>, cut away so the inner ones are visible.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Three concentric spheres of radii one, two and three, drawn cut away so
that the smaller spheres inside can be seen. Each sphere is a level
surface on which the function x squared plus y squared plus z squared is
constant.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
% three cut-away hemispheres, drawn in the picture plane
\begin{scope}
  % outer, radius 3 -> drawn at 2.4cm
  \fill[cyan!30, opacity=0.55] (-2.4,0) arc (180:360:2.4cm) arc (0:180:2.4cm and 0.62cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-2.4,0) arc (180:360:2.4cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-2.4,0) arc (180:0:2.4cm and 0.62cm);
  % middle, radius 2 -> 1.6cm
  \fill[orange!35, opacity=0.6] (-1.6,0) arc (180:360:1.6cm) arc (0:180:1.6cm and 0.42cm);
  \draw[orange!60!black] (-1.6,0) arc (180:360:1.6cm);
  \draw[orange!60!black] (-1.6,0) arc (180:0:1.6cm and 0.42cm);
  % inner, radius 1 -> 0.8cm
  \fill[green!45, opacity=0.75] (-0.8,0) arc (180:360:0.8cm) arc (0:180:0.8cm and 0.2cm);
  \draw[green!45!black] (-0.8,0) arc (180:360:0.8cm);
  \draw[green!45!black] (-0.8,0) arc (180:0:0.8cm and 0.2cm);
\end{scope}
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,3.1) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (3.3,-0.85) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.9,-1.3) node[below left] {$x$};
% labels
\node[green!35!black, anchor=west]  at (2.6,1.9) {$x^2+y^2+z^2=1$};
\node[orange!60!black, anchor=west] at (2.6,1.35) {$x^2+y^2+z^2=4$};
\node[cyan!55!black, anchor=west]   at (2.6,0.8) {$x^2+y^2+z^2=9$};
\draw[gray, thin] (2.55,1.9) -- (0.42,0.42);
\draw[gray, thin] (2.55,1.35) -- (1.05,0.95);
\draw[gray, thin] (2.55,0.8) -- (1.75,1.5);
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
For functions of four or more variables we can no longer visualize the
graph or the level surfaces, and we must rely on intuition developed
through the study of functions of two and three variables.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-fsv-level-surfaces">
<title>Level Surfaces of a Function of Three Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Describe the level surfaces of <m>g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 - z^2</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Three cases, decided by the sign of <m>c</m>. Take <m>c = 0</m> first and
solve for <m>z</m>. For <m>c \neq 0</m> the surfaces are the hyperboloids
of <xref ref="subsec-skel-hyperboloids"/>; decide the number of sheets not
by drawing but by asking, for each <m>c</m>, which values of <m>z</m> are
possible <mdash/> rearrange so that one side is a square and see whether it
can vanish.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {$c = 0$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$c \gt 0$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {$c \lt 0$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three labelled rows, one for each sign of the constant c, for describing the corresponding level surface.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-fsv-hyperboloids">
<caption>
Level surfaces of <m>g(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2-z^2</m>. For <m>c \gt 0</m> the
surface is a hyperboloid of one sheet, for <m>c = 0</m> it is a cone, and
for <m>c \lt 0</m> it is a hyperboloid of two sheets.
</caption>
<image width="98%">
<description>
<p>
Three surfaces side by side. On the left a hyperboloid of one sheet, a
connected surface pinched in at the middle like a cooling tower. In the
middle a double cone meeting at a point at the origin. On the right a
hyperboloid of two sheets, consisting of two separate bowls, one opening
upwards above the origin and one opening downwards below it.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
% ============ left: one sheet, c > 0 ============
\begin{axis}[
  name=one,
  width=6.4cm, height=7.0cm,
  view={-35}{16},
  xmin=-2.2, xmax=2.2, ymin=-2.2, ymax=2.2, zmin=-1.8, zmax=1.8,
  hide axis, clip=false,
]
% x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1  ->  r = sqrt(1 + z^2)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=cyan!35, draw=cyan!55!black,
          fill opacity=0.55, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=-1.5:1.5, y domain=0:360, samples=16, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({sqrt(1+x^2)*cos(y)},{sqrt(1+x^2)*sin(y)},{x});
\node at (axis cs:0,0,-2.5) {$g(x,y,z)=c\;\;(c>0)$};
\end{axis}
% ============ middle: the cone, c = 0 ============
\begin{axis}[
  name=cone, at={(one.east)}, anchor=west, xshift=2mm,
  width=6.4cm, height=7.0cm,
  view={-35}{16},
  xmin=-2.2, xmax=2.2, ymin=-2.2, ymax=2.2, zmin=-1.8, zmax=1.8,
  hide axis, clip=false,
]
% x^2 + y^2 = z^2  ->  r = |z|
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=orange!30, draw=orange!60!black,
          fill opacity=0.55, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=-1.7:1.7, y domain=0:360, samples=16, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({abs(x)*cos(y)},{abs(x)*sin(y)},{x});
\node at (axis cs:0,0,-2.5) {$g(x,y,z)=0$};
\end{axis}
% ============ right: two sheets, c < 0 ============
\begin{axis}[
  name=two, at={(cone.east)}, anchor=west, xshift=2mm,
  width=6.4cm, height=7.0cm,
  view={-35}{16},
  xmin=-2.2, xmax=2.2, ymin=-2.2, ymax=2.2, zmin=-1.8, zmax=1.8,
  hide axis, clip=false,
]
% x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = -1  ->  r = sqrt(z^2 - 1),  |z| >= 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=green!35, draw=green!45!black,
          fill opacity=0.6, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=1:1.75, y domain=0:360, samples=12, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({sqrt(x^2-1)*cos(y)},{sqrt(x^2-1)*sin(y)},{x});
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=green!35, draw=green!45!black,
          fill opacity=0.6, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=-1.75:-1, y domain=0:360, samples=12, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({sqrt(x^2-1)*cos(y)},{sqrt(x^2-1)*sin(y)},{x});
\node at (axis cs:0,0,-2.5) {$g(x,y,z)=c\;\;(c<0)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
